Trial patch (item 5): tail-skip for the MoE slot runtime — skip the
install and the compute of a verify-batch expert that is routed only
at the last rank, and count its contribution as zero for that step.

Everything here is confined to src/llama-moe-slot.cpp: candidate
selection and slot-id assignment in process(), a reserved zero slot
in the per-layer decode window, and the state and telemetry those
need. The graph is NOT touched, and does not need to be: in this fork
the graph reads router weights by the original expert ids and expert
weights through the slot ids this runtime writes, so the slot-id
tensor is the only contract between the two sides (see
TAILSKIP_SCOPE.md section 3).

The rule (env LLAMA_MOE_TAIL_SKIP=1, default OFF):

  In a DSpark verify batch (window mode, more than one row), a
  missing (non-resident) expert routed ONLY at the last rank
  (rank == n_used-1, i.e. rank 7 of the top-8) by every row of the
  batch is skipped for that step: it is not installed, and the
  positions that routed it get the reserved zero slot as their slot
  id. That slot's decode bindings (gate/up/down) are memset to zero
  when the slot is armed, so the graph computes
  silu(0)*0 = 0 and down @ 0 = 0 — the contribution is exactly the
  zero vector, whatever the router weight. If any row routes the
  expert at ranks 0..n_used-2 it installs normally. Prefill never
  enters window mode and single-row decode is excluded by the
  n_rows > 1 gate: verify batches only.

Rank is read from the flattened id tensor exactly where the fork
already reads it for the A2 deferral: pos = rank + n_used * row, so
rank = pos % n_used with n_used = expert_ids->ne[0].

The zero slot is the LAST slot of the layer's window. It is armed
lazily, the first time a step in a window phase actually skips:
any resident there is evicted through the normal mapping tables,
the slot contents are zeroed, and from then on the allocation range
in process() is clamped to exclude it and claim_slot_in_window
skips it, until clear_layer_cache disarms it at the next phase
transition. Disclosed cost: while armed the window has one fewer
usable slot (31 of 32); a later batch in the same generation that
needed 32 installs with no skippable expert would hit the fork's
own fit guard. Observed verify batches demand ~17-24 unique experts
per layer, far from that line.

Mechanically, a skipped expert is marked resident at the zero slot
in expert_to_slot just before the demand loop, so the loop's stock
resident branch assigns the zero slot to its positions — the demand
loop itself carries no tail-skip code. The markings are restored
immediately after the loop, before the n_unique accounting.

Accounting stays honest: a skipped expert is neither an install
nor a hit. It stays in requested[] during the demand loop (victim
scans see true routing) and is subtracted from n_unique afterward,
so the flush lines' hit = unique - miss stays truthful. Telemetry
is three impl atomics, printed once at teardown like the lookahead
report: "tail-skip: batches=%llu skipped=%llu bytes=%.1fMiB"
(batches = layer calls with at least one skip; bytes = skipped x
the layer's decode per-expert byte sum, the reads that did not
happen).

This patch CHANGES OUTPUTS when the flag is on (a routed expert's
contribution is dropped) — that is the lever being tested, judged
on fluency and a structured-output probe, not token-id equality.
With the flag off, every new branch is untaken and the runtime is
behavior-identical to the unpatched one: the flag member is false,
no candidate set is built, the clamp reads an armed flag that is
never set, and the n_unique adjustment subtracts zero.

